think With encaps GREEN and sent, the next Lindy-first crux is decapsulation — it closes the KEM round-trip (keygen → encaps → decaps) and composes the GREEN encaps for the Fujisaki–Okamoto re-encryption verify. Read Zig std.crypto ml_kem InnerSk.decrypt (m = v − InvNTT(s_hat ∘ NTT(u)), compress(1)) and SecretKey.decaps (G(m'‖H(pk)), re-encrypt, cmov to rejection secret K̄ = SHAKE256(z‖c) on ciphertext mismatch) to author the standard-domain equivalent.
think Authored crypto/mlkem_decaps.rye: k_pke_decrypt (decompress u and v from the ciphertext segments, decode s_hat from dk_pke, w = v − InvNTT(s_hat ∘ NTT(u)) via canonical (v+q−x)%q subtraction, m = ByteEncode_1(Compress_1(w))) and decaps (parse dk into dk_pke/ek/H(ek)/z, decrypt to m', G-derive (K',r'), re-encrypt through encaps.k_pke_encrypt, accept K' on byte-equal c else K̄). Wrote the branch plainly since inputs are public test vectors and timing-safety is a measured horizon, noted in the module head.
think The selftest proves BOTH branches against Zig's SecretKey.decaps: honest ciphertext must recover exactly the encaps shared secret K AND equal Zig; a one-byte-flipped ciphertext must equal Zig's derived rejection secret AND differ from K (proving the reject branch actually fired). Refused trivial all-zero and non-distinct secrets.
obs GREEN on the FIRST build across all three parameter sets over 8 seeds each, both branches — the full KEM keygen → encaps → decaps round-trip now agrees with the independent Zig implementation end to end, including the implicit-rejection path that makes ML-KEM CCA-secure.
obs TAME style bans clean; every function carries contract asserts. README synced same round with the sixth ML-KEM rung row.
